# Tests run on the Windows (MSYS2/UCRT64) CI build, see .CI/common.groovy's
# sanityCheck() and testsuite/runWindowsTests.sh.
#
# We do not run the whole testsuite on Windows: it is roughly ten times slower
# there (https://github.com/OpenModelica/OpenModelica/issues/16279). This is a
# smoke set instead - enough to catch a broken toolchain, a missing DLL or a
# path handling bug, not to check compiler semantics, which the Linux runs
# already cover.
#
# Every test here must run with nothing but an installed omc: no library from
# libs-for-testing (the Windows job git-cleans the workspace, so
# libraries/.openmodelica is empty) and no file from reference-files. The one
# exception is FFITestLib, which runWindowsTests.sh builds first.
#
# Paths are relative to the testsuite root, one per line; '#' starts a comment.

# MetaModelica code generation and the C compile of the generated code.
./metamodelica/meta/AlgPatternm.mos

# External C, the Windows failure everybody hits: finding the shared library.
# countLines goes to ModelicaExternalC, which ships with omc; Integer1 goes to
# FFITestLib.dll, which we build beside the test.
./flattening/modelica/ffi/ModelicaInternal_countLines.mos
./flattening/modelica/ffi/Integer1.mos

# An external C library compiled and linked while building the simulation, and
# a model calling LAPACK - i.e. the Fortran side of the toolchain.
./simulation/modelica/external_functions/ExternalLibraries.mos
./simulation/modelica/external_functions/LapackInverse.mos

# C runtime: a nonlinear system solved by KINSOL, so the SUNDIALS libraries
# have to be there and usable.
./simulation/modelica/nonlinear_system/problem1_kinsol.mos

# Linearization, which writes and reads back a generated Modelica file.
./openmodelica/linearization/simVanDerPol.mos

# C++ runtime: events, a .mat result file and comparing it against a reference
# checked into the test directory.
./openmodelica/cppruntime/BouncingBall.mos

# FMI export. The 2.0 test links against libfmilib, zips the FMU, imports it
# back and simulates it; the 3.0 one exports arrays with the new backend. A
# co-simulation test would be worth adding once this stage is green, but it
# needs OMSimulator to run the FMU.
./openmodelica/fmi/ModelExchange/2.0/HelloFMIWorld.mos
./openmodelica/fmi/ModelExchange/3.0/fmi3_arrays.mos
